There is no mathematics on this page: no formulas, derivations or computations. The folio is a set of excerpts that Riemann copied from J. F. Herbart's works. The references (W. III = Hartenstein edition, vol. III, Allgemeine Metaphysik) follow the same excerpting pattern as the previous page, which quotes Herbart's Psychologie als Wissenschaft and Metaphysik. The page has three excerpts.

(1) Met. I §79, W. III, 219. Herbart argues that modern chemistry would have forced Leibniz to make some concession to a causa transiens (transeunt causation between substances). Merely moving bodies around can be described without any inner change in the monads, but in chemistry nearly all the qualities by which a thing is known change when two substances combine. Combination must therefore reach deeper than spatial position: it is a 'Durchdringung' (interpenetration) that is more than spatial. Yet each component can re-emerge unchanged afterwards. So no 'fabulous transitus and influxus' takes place in which a thing loses its own quality.

(2) Allgemeine Anmerkung zum dritten Capitel of the historical part. Herbart names the 'main error' of almost all schools except the Eleatics: splitting Being into a prior possibility plus a later complement (complementum possibilitatis). He traces it historically:
- Plato's quality-less matter, a mere possibility that is formed according to the Ideas;
- Aristotle's wavering between matter, form and the composite, with a tendency to favour form;
- the scholastic essentiae rerum as bearers of possibility;
- Spinoza's substance as 'possibility sent ahead of things';
- the Leibniz–Wolff school and the Schelling school as continuations of this error.

(3) Schlussanmerkung zum ersten Theil, W. III, 492. Herbart reads Plato's Timaeus on the receptacle. Plato saw that absolutely posited qualities (the Ideas) cannot explain change in the world. Things therefore need a 'mother' as well as a 'father': a receptive, shapeless substrate without any quality. Plato himself calls this as if dreaming of it. The motive was his division between knowledge (of the unchanging) and opinion. Once the contradiction in change had been found but not resolved, sensible things could only be appearances. Since they nevertheless resemble the Ideas, there must be an underlying something able to take on and lose all opposite properties. It may therefore have no nature of its own. The next page continues: this substrate is 'possibility itself'.

These excerpts document Riemann's study of Herbart's metaphysics: the problem of change, causa transiens versus Leibnizian monads, and the critique of 'possibility before being'. That study bears on his own Naturphilosophie fragments, though nothing here is his own argument.
